# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF, IN THE INDEX AND ON DISK.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXES, MEASURED. A FRESH CLONE OF THIS REPO ON WINDOWS FAILED ITS
# OWN TEST SUITE. core.autocrlf is true on the machine this project runs on and
# nothing overrode it, so a new clone or `git worktree add` checked every text
# file out as CRLF -- while the long-lived clone, whose files were written by
# tools rather than by checkout, held them as LF. The two disagreed by accident
# of history: 139 LF-only files and 106 containing CRLF in the same working
# copy.
#
# test/deployCheckTree.test.mjs builds a fake "live" bundle to compare against,
# and does it by converting LF to CRLF in the repo's own source. Handed a file
# that is ALREADY CRLF that produces a doubled carriage return, so every line
# differs, and two assertions fail -- on a clean checkout, with nobody having
# changed anything. A suite that is red out of the box is one people learn to
# ignore, and this repository has written down twice what that costs.
#
# eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf per repository, so it holds regardless of how
# any given machine is configured. That matters here because this project is
# checked out many times on one Windows box -- fifteen worktrees at the time of
# writing -- and half of them were getting different bytes than the other half.
#
# ADDING THIS DOES NOT FLOOD `git status`, which was the reason to hesitate and
# it was checked rather than assumed. `text=auto` cleans CRLF to LF when git
# reads a working file, so existing CRLF checkouts compare equal to the index
# and show nothing modified. Probed on a fully-CRLF worktree before committing:
# 0 tracked files reported modified. Files already on disk keep their CRLF until
# something checks them out again; that is cosmetic, and any re-checkout fixes
# it.
#
# The same rule, and the same reason, as the social-sparks repo beside this one,
# where an unpinned eol produced 48,607 phantom lint errors in one afternoon.
* text=auto eol=lf
